A board bound to `0.0.0.0` — which is still the default, so the phone still works — now comes up with a token, and every URL it prints carries `?t=`. Bound to `127.0.0.1` it stays open, because nothing but you can reach it.

The insecure-by-default state is no longer reachable by accident. It is still reachable **on purpose**: `--no-token` serves it open on a network you trust.

The rule is one expression, and the order matters — an explicit flag always beats the inference:

```js
const want = args['no-token'] ? false
  : typeof args.token === 'string' ? args.token   // --token X
  : args.token ? true                            // --token
  : !LOOPBACK.has(host);                         // the new bit
```

The token is **persisted on the board**, which was already true — so restarting does not invalidate a link already open on a phone. I checked that specifically, because it is the one way this change could have quietly hurt.

Also: it says why. An unexplained token in a URL reads as a bug, so the first run prints `minted because this binds 0.0.0.0, not loopback` and names both escape hatches.

**Files** — `board.mjs:1234-1258`, `board.mjs:1300-1310`

